<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bill of Rights 1689</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bill of Rights 1689 is an Act of the English Parliament that set out the terms on which William III and Mary II took the throne after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>. It declared illegal the suspension of laws and taxation without Parliament's consent, forbade a standing army in peacetime without its approval, guaranteed free elections and freedom of speech in Parliament, and barred Catholics from the throne. It remains central to the British constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689.jpg" alt="Bill of Rights 1689" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_England_(1689%E2%80%931694).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a> revived in the 17th century, and Parliament passed the Petition of Right in 1628 and the Habeas Corpus Act in 1679. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> and the years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> and the Restoration, Parliamentarians who objected to the policies of James II invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> stadtholder William III of Orange-Nassau to overthrow him. William's invasion led James to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in December 1688 the peers appointed William provisional governor. A Convention Parliament convened on 22 January 1689.</p><p>On 29 January the House of Commons proposed a statement of the rights James had violated. On 13 February the Marquess of Halifax offered the throne to William and Mary, who accepted, and they were crowned on 11 April under a new oath promising to govern according to the statutes agreed in Parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Declaration of Right was enacted as the Bill of Rights, which received royal assent on 16 December 1689. It declared illegal the suspension of laws or dispensing with them without Parliament's consent, the commission for ecclesiastical causes, and the levying of taxes without a grant of Parliament. The Bill affirmed the right to petition the king, free elections, freedom of speech in Parliament, and frequent parliaments. It held that a standing army in peacetime without Parliament's consent was against the law, and that Protestants might have arms for their defence as allowed by law. Excessive bail and cruel and unusual punishments were also forbidden.</p><p>The Act listed twelve policies of James II said to subvert the Protestant religion and the laws of the kingdom, including the prosecution of the Seven Bishops, and declared that he had abdicated. It barred Roman Catholics from the throne, and named William and Mary as successors to James, with the crown passing then to Mary's heirs and to her sister, Princess Anne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> of 1701 later altered the line of succession. A separate Claim of Right Act of 1689 applies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Together they contributed to parliamentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, and William Blackstone described such acts as fundamental laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The Bill influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia-declaration-of-rights">Virginia Declaration of Rights</a> of 1776, and the Eighth Amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> reproduces almost verbatim its ban on excessive bail and cruel and unusual punishment. It was also a model for the French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen and the European Convention on Human Rights.</p><p>The Bill of Rights remains in statute in the United Kingdom and other Commonwealth realms, and Article 9 on freedom of speech in Parliament is still cited. It restates in statutory form the Declaration of Right that the Convention Parliament presented in February 1689, inviting William III and Mary II to become joint sovereigns.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bill of Rights 1689” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conflict between the Stuart kings and Parliament led to civil war, the execution of Charles I and a republic, and the Glorious Revolution of 1688 and the <b>Bill of Rights</b> of 1689 confirmed parliamentary supremacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The struggle against absolutism produced its opposites: the <b>Bill of Rights 1689</b> in England, Locke’s theory of government by consent, Montesquieu’s separation of powers and the United States Declaration of Independence, which accused George III of seeking “an absolute Tyranny over these States”.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Declaration of Right, enacted in December as the <b>Bill of Rights 1689</b>, forbade the crown to suspend laws, levy taxes or keep a standing army in peacetime without Parliament’s consent, and required free elections and freedom of speech in Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…the 1640s and the Glorious Revolution of 1688 settled that the Crown could not rule without Parliament: the <b>Bill of Rights 1689</b> forbade taxation and a standing army in peacetime without its consent and protected free speech in its debates, and because money and military discipline were voted…</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bill of Rights 1689</b>, enacted after the Glorious Revolution, forbade the monarch to suspend laws, levy taxes or keep a standing army in peacetime without Parliament's consent, and constitutional monarchy came to mean a crown that reigned while ministers answerable to Parliament governed, keeping, in Walter Bagehot's…</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li></ul></section>
